  "final_audio_transcript": " This diving bell spider lives underwater inside a chamber of air it builds, but how can it  breathe there?  It spins silk among water plants, then brings bubbles down from the surface to fill its  bell.  The spider needs oxygen.  A single trapped pocket of air would not last forever on its own.  Here is the surprise.  Oxygen dissolved in the surrounding water diffuses into the bell through its surface.  Scientists measured oxygen inside and outside these bells, alongside the needs of resting  In warm aquarium water, many bells took in enough oxygen to support a spider at rest.  But other gas, especially nitrogen, leaks away.  The bell shrinks, and air must eventually be replaced.  This does not prove the bell supplies an active spider in every pond or every season.  It is a physical gill for a resting spider.  The water helps keep its tiny underwater home breathable.",
